.TH BLTZR 1 "October 9, 2026" "bltzr" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bltzr \- extract vocabulary for language study
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bltzr
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-V | \-\-version ]
.br
.B bltzr blitz \-l
.I CODE
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B bltzr list-languages
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B bltzr install-plugin
.I CODE_OR_DIRECTORY
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B bltzr remove-plugin
.I CODE
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B bltzr cleanup-known
.I CODE PATH
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B bltzr history
.I CODE
.RI [ TERM ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B bltzr dev
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
bltzr extracts unique vocabulary from UTF-8 text, optionally displays
basic words (lexeme/lemma), skips selected words, counts surviving occurrences,
and selects example sentences. Language packs provide dictionary data,
normalization rules and language-specific word boundaries.
.PP
Basic mode (code
.BR base )
and bundled English
.RB ( eng )
are available without downloading a pack. Other languages require an
installed pack. Processing works offline; installing a registered pack
downloads data from GitHub. Report prompts are exported text, not API calls.
.PP
Vocabulary goes to standard output; diagnostics and update summaries go
to standard error. An ambiguous input form can produce several lemma
candidates. bltzr does not choose candidates based on sentence meaning.
Dictionary coverage and word-boundary support vary by language.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B blitz
Extract vocabulary. Requires
.BR \-l / \-\-language .
Read text from
.BR \-t / \-\-text ,
a UTF-8 file selected with
.BR \-i / \-\-file ,
or piped standard input. Text and file options cannot be combined.
Explicit text or a file takes precedence over piped input.
Empty or whitespace-only input is an error.
.TP
.B list-languages
List installed languages, including base and bundled English.
Output is Name (code), sorted alphabetically by name.
.TP
.B list-available-languages
List every registered downloadable language, base mode and bundled English
in the same format and order. Uses the packaged registry; works offline
without reading user configuration or installed packs.
.TP
.B config, conf
Open the selected user configuration in VISUAL or EDITOR (or the default
terminal editor). Create a minimal config when absent; preserve existing files.
Accepts \-C/\-\-config PATH and respects BLITZER_CONFIG.
.TP
.BI "install-plugin " CODE_OR_DIRECTORY
Install a registered language code or a local pack directory.
Use
.BR \-R / \-\-replace
to update an existing pack. Known lists and history are preserved.
.TP
.BI "remove-plugin " CODE
Remove a user-installed pack after confirmation.
.BR \-y / \-\-yes
skips the confirmation. Bundled English remains available as a fallback.
.TP
.BI "cleanup-known " "CODE PATH"
Preview normalization and duplicate removal for the specified word-list
file. Print a JSON summary. With
.BR \-a / \-\-apply ,
rewrite the file, preserving comments and first-seen term order,
and save the previous contents as
.IR PATH.bak .
.TP
.BI "history " "CODE " "[TERM]"
Print saved example sentences as JSON. An optional term matches the exact
stored display term: for example, help when lemmas were displayed, or
helps when forms were displayed. Use
.BR \-m / \-\-limit
to set the maximum number of rows (default 100, minimum 1).
Missing history returns an empty list.
.TP
.B dev
Pack development commands; see PACK DEVELOPMENT below.
.SH COMMON COMMAND OPTIONS
These options follow the selected command, not the program name.
They apply to blitz, list-languages, install-plugin, remove-plugin,
cleanup-known, history and the pack development commands.
.TP
.BI "\-C, \-\-config " PATH
Read this TOML configuration file instead of automatic discovery.
An explicitly selected missing file is an error.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-no-config
Use built-in defaults without reading a configuration file.
Cannot be combined with \-\-config.
.TP
.BI "\-P, \-\-language-packs-dir " DIRECTORY
Override the configured language-pack directory. The old \-\-plugins-dir
flag remains an alias.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help for the selected command. At the program level,
.BR \-V / \-\-version
prints the application version.
.SH BLITZ OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-language " CODE
Required. Use a three-letter lowercase language-pack code, or base.
.TP
.BI "\-t, \-\-text " TEXT
Process explicit text. Quote it to keep spaces together.
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-file " PATH
Read a UTF-8 input file.
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-lemmatize / \-\-no-lemmatize
Show basic words (lexeme/lemma) instead of words as written (word form):
are becomes be, and cats becomes cat. Unknown words remain visible unless
excluded. Display never changes what either skip list removes.
The built-in default is \-\-no-lemmatize.
.TP
.B \-x, \-\-exclude-unknown / \-\-include-unknown
Hide or include words without dictionary matches. Default: include.
Base mode does not support excluding unknown words.
.TP
.BI "\-\-skip-exact-words-file " PATH
Skip only the words listed in this file (word form).
For to be, listing be and am skips only those two words; is, are, was,
were, being and been can still be counted. Listing cat leaves cats counted.
Replaces only the configured exact-word file, leaving the family file active.
.TP
.BI "\-\-skip-word-families-file " PATH
Skip listed word families (lexeme/lemma), using the dictionary.
For to be, list be, without "to", to skip be, am, is, are, was, were,
being and been. Listing cat skips cat and cats.
Replaces only the configured family file, leaving the exact-word file active.
.TP
.B \-N, \-\-show-all-words
Apply neither skip list for this run. Cannot be combined with explicit
skip-file options. This affects skipping, not a requested list update;
use \-\-no-update-known to disable additions.
.TP
.BI "\-\-update-list " exact-words|word-families
Explicitly choose which file receives additions when \-\-update-known or
\-\-test-known is used. Requires the corresponding skip-file setting or
option. Exact-word updates add encountered spellings (word form);
word-family updates add dictionary basic words (lexeme/lemma).
The choice is independent of display.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-freq / \-\-no-freq
Include surviving occurrence counts in text, TSV and report output.
Default: off. JSON always includes counts.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-context / \-\-no-context
Include example sentences in output. Default: off.
Only surviving occurrences supply examples; the matched input form is
highlighted even when the displayed entry is a lemma.
.TP
.BI "\-m, \-\-context-limit " NUMBER
Retain up to this many examples per displayed entry, from 1 to 20.
Default: 2. Repeated identical sentences do not fill additional slots.
.TP
.BI "\-d, \-\-sentence-pattern " REGEX
Python regular expression matching sentence-ending delimiters.
It must consume text rather than match empty boundaries.
The built-in pattern recognizes several scripts' sentence punctuation
and newlines. Original sentence text and source offsets are preserved.
.TP
.BI "\-b, \-\-bold " off|html|markdown|org
Highlight matched words in examples. Default: html.
The off setting keeps original text; JSON always uses plain text and offsets.
.TP
.BI "\-S, \-\-sort " ORDER
Sort by textual-frequency (default), alphabetical, appearance,
global-frequency, or custom. See SORTING.
.TP
.BI "\-O, \-\-custom-order " PATH
Ranking file for custom sorting: one normalized term per line,
highest priority first. Unlisted terms follow alphabetically.
.TP
.BI "\-o, \-\-format " text|tsv|json|report
Choose output format. Default: text. See OUTPUT FORMATS.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-prompt / \-\-no-prompt
Include the configured language's prompt_text in a report's PROMPT
section. Requires report format and nonempty prompt_text.
Default: off. The report can be copied into another tool; bltzr does
not send it anywhere or invoke an AI service.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-src / \-\-no-src
Include original input in a report's SOURCE section.
Requires report format. Default: off.
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-save-context / \-\-no-save-context
Explicitly save or decline saving surviving examples to history,
overriding the configured save_context policy.
Saving collects examples even when \-\-context output is off.
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-update-known / \-\-no-update-known
Enable or disable additions to the explicitly chosen skip list.
Default: off. Choose \-\-update-list exact-words or word-families, or set
update_list in the language configuration. Only dictionary-matched surviving
words propose additions; only the chosen list is written. Missing files
are created when there are additions. Existing files are backed up as PATH.bak
when rewritten. Reading a word does not mean you know it.
.TP
.B \-T, \-\-test-known
Preview proposed additions to the explicitly chosen skip list on standard
error. Write neither list nor history, even if saving or updating
was otherwise enabled. Vocabulary still goes to standard output.
.SH CHOOSE WHICH WORDS TO SKIP
There are two optional files. Each has one job. Use either or both.
Skipped occurrences contribute nothing to vocabulary counts or examples.
.SS Skip these exact words (word form)
Use skip_exact_words_file when you know some spellings but still want to
study others. For the verb to be, you might know be and am, but still want
to study is, are, was, were, being and been.
.PP
Put these words in the file:
.PP
.nf
be
am
cat
.fi
.PP
Only be, am and cat are skipped. The basic word be has no special meaning
in this list: it does not skip any other form. Cats can still be counted.
For Slovenian, listing sem and biti here leaves sva counted.
.SS Skip these words and their other forms: word families (lexeme/lemma)
Use skip_word_families_file when you want to skip a word and all its
other grammatical forms recognized by the dictionary. For to be, put be
in the file, without "to". Put cat in the file to skip both cat and cats:
.PP
.nf
be
cat
.fi
.PP
The be family skips be, am, is, are, was, were, being and been.
The cat family skips cat and cats, but not a related word such as catlike.
Write basic words here: be rather than am, and cat rather than cats.
The program does not guess a whole family from an inflected entry.
.SS Comparison
.nf
List             Entry    Skipped              Still counted
Exact words      be, am   be, am               is, are, was, were,
                                              being, been
Word families    be       be, am, is, are,     other words
                          was, were, being,
                          been
Exact words      cat      cat                  cats
Word families    cat      cat, cats            other words
.fi
.SS Display does not change either list
\-L/\-\-lemmatize shows basic words (lexeme/lemma); without it, output
shows words as written (word form). If be and am are in the exact-word
file, are still contributes a count even when \-L displays it as be.
The word-family file skips are if it contains be, regardless of display.
.SS Files and dictionary coverage
Each setting names one file, not a word or an array of files. Omit settings
you do not need. Both files are UTF-8 with one word per line. Blank lines
and whole-line # comments are allowed. Surrounding whitespace is trimmed;
internal whitespace is rejected. Hyphenated words are accepted, including
ordinary and Unicode hyphens. Entries use the language pack's normalization,
usually ignoring capital letters. Acceptance of hyphenated entries does not
change the tokenizer's word boundaries in input text.
.PP
Missing files are treated as empty lists. Ordinary extraction never creates
or updates them. Basic mode supports exact words; families need a dictionary.
Dictionary coverage determines which other forms are recognized.
.PP
The bundled English dictionary currently lacks some links in the to be
family. The examples describe the grammatical relationship; a pack must
contain the links to skip that family automatically. With current bundled
data, put all eight spellings in the exact-word file to skip them all.
Skip-list settings do not repair missing dictionary data.
.PP
A spelling can belong to several families. Saw can mean a tool or a past
form of see. Listing see removes its candidate, but the saw tool candidate
can remain. The program does not choose the meaning from the sentence.
.SS Command-line replacement
Each new file option replaces only its corresponding configured file.
The other list stays active. Selecting new file options uses the new lists
instead of configured legacy files for that run. \-\-show-all-words applies
neither list; it cannot be combined with explicit skip-file options.
.SS Updating the files
Updates stay off by default. Choose the destination with
\-\-update-list exact-words or word-families. \-\-test-known previews
additions without writing either list or history; \-\-update-known writes
to the chosen list only. The selection does not follow \-L.
.SH SORTING
.TP
.B textual-frequency
Descending surviving occurrence count, then spelling.
.TP
.B alphabetical
Displayed term spelling.
.TP
.B appearance
First surviving source position, then spelling.
.TP
.B global-frequency
Descending pack frequency, then spelling; terms without a frequency
follow those with a frequency. Requires pack frequency data and is
unavailable in base mode.
.TP
.B custom
Order from custom_order or \-\-custom-order. Unlisted terms follow
alphabetically. The file uses the known-list format and normalization.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.TP
.B text
One entry per line. Optional count and context columns are tab-separated.
Fields containing tabs or newlines are quoted using CSV conventions.
.TP
.B tsv
The same rows with a header: term, optional count, optional context.
Multiple examples share the context cell, joined by <br> for HTML
highlighting or " | " for other styles.
.TP
.B json
A JSON array of objects containing term and count, optional
global_frequency, and, when requested, contexts. Each context has text,
highlight_start and highlight_end; offsets refer to the original sentence
and the end is exclusive. Markup is not embedded.
.TP
.B report
Optional PROMPT and SOURCE sections followed by VOCABULARY.
Vocabulary uses the text format. Suitable for copying into a study tool
or an external assistant; no request is sent by bltzr.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are UTF-8 TOML. Precedence, from highest to lowest, is explicit
command-line options, [languages.CODE], [defaults], and built-in defaults.
Unknown fields and invalid values are errors.
.PP
Configuration-file selection is: \-\-config PATH, then BLITZER_CONFIG,
then $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bltzr/bltzr.toml, using ~/.config/bltzr/bltzr.toml
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset or empty. These defaults apply on all platforms.
Relative XDG roots are ignored. No discovered file means built-in defaults.
Only the explicit config/conf command creates a configuration file.
.PP
Configured paths are relative to the configuration file and support
~ and environment-variable expansion. Unset variables in paths are errors.
Command-line paths are relative to the current working directory.
.SS Locations
[locations] accepts language_packs_dir (pack directory) and history_file
(SQLite sentence history). The old plugins_dir key remains an alias;
do not specify both names. The default pack directory is
$XDG_DATA_HOME/bltzr/languages, or ~/.local/share/bltzr/languages.
The default history file is $XDG_STATE_HOME/bltzr/contexts.db,
or ~/.local/state/bltzr/contexts.db. Omit these settings to respect XDG roots.
A pack directory override affects both installation and processing.
Files from the former platform data directory are not moved automatically;
explicit location settings can retain them.
.SS Defaults and language overrides
[defaults] accepts lemmatize, exclude_unknown, freq, context,
format, sort, bold, context_limit, sentence_pattern, save_context,
auto_update_known, prompt, src and custom_order.
Defaults and choices are described under BLITZ OPTIONS.
The configuration equivalent of \-\-update-known is auto_update_known.
.PP
save_context accepts flag-only (default: save only when requested),
always, prompt (interactive confirmation), or never.
Explicit \-\-save-context or \-\-no-save-context overrides any policy.
With prompt policy, pipelines must explicitly select one of these flags
so that standard input remains vocabulary input.
.PP
[languages.CODE] can override all defaults and additionally accepts
skip_exact_words_file, skip_word_families_file, update_list and prompt_text.
The two skip files have fixed meanings; no filter mode is needed. To enable
configured automatic additions, explicitly set update_list to exact-words
or word-families and select the corresponding file.
prompt_text is optional instructions for a report's PROMPT section;
it is only used when prompt=true or \-\-prompt is selected.
It does not change extraction or contact an external service.
.SS Example
.nf
[locations]
plugins_dir = "./language-packs"
history_file = "./contexts.db"

[defaults]
lemmatize = true
context = true
save_context = "flag-only"

[languages.slv]
skip_exact_words_file = "./slv-exact-words.txt"
skip_word_families_file = "./slv-word-families.txt"
# update_list = "exact-words"
prompt_text = "Prepare vocabulary study cards."
.fi
.SH MIGRATING OLDER SETTINGS
Existing configurations and scripts retain their original behavior.
Legacy options are hidden from normal CLI help but remain accepted.
Do not mix old file settings or a language-level filter_by with new file
settings in the same language section. Explicit old CLI filtering options
cannot be combined with new skip-file selections.
.PP
Old known_file and exclusions files with filter_by="forms" belong in the
exact-word list (word form). With filter_by="lemmas", they belong in the
word-family list (lexeme/lemma): review entries and put basic words there;
move inflected entries intended for exact skipping into the exact-word file.
Old forms_only files belong in the exact-word list. If several old files
belong to the same new list, combine their contents into one file.
.PP
Remove old file keys and language-level filter_by when selecting the new
settings. A legacy filter_by in [defaults] does not change the new lists.
No configuration or word files are migrated automatically.
.SS Legacy options
\-F/\-\-filter-by forms|lemmas selects the old known-file matching mode.
\-k/\-\-known-file PATH selects a primary known list for reading and updating.
\-e/\-\-exclude PATH is repeatable and replaces configured exclusions,
suppressing the configured known_file for reading unless explicitly supplied.
\-E/\-\-exclude-forms PATH is repeatable and replaces configured forms_only.
\-\-no-exclusions is an alias for \-\-show-all-words.
.PP
In the old interface, exact listed input forms always remove occurrences;
lemmas mode additionally removes listed dictionary candidates. Old additional
files must exist; a missing primary known_file is empty. Old updates use
known_file as the destination, with additions determined by filter_by.
Legacy settings never change the fixed meanings of the new files.
.SH HISTORY
Saving stores the language, displayed term, original sentence, highlight
offsets and first-seen timestamp. Repeated identical language/term/context
records are deduplicated. Only surviving vocabulary contributes records.
History is separate from both skip lists and never makes words known or
removes them from vocabulary.
.SH PACK DEVELOPMENT
The dev group is available through bltzr dev --help, even though it is
hidden from the top-level command list. These commands accept the common
configuration and pack-directory options.
.TP
.BI "dev build-plugin " SOURCE_DIR
Build and validate a pack from config.toml and forms.tsv (form and lemma
columns), with optional frequencies.tsv and attribution files.
\-D/\-\-database PATH imports a compatible SQLite database instead.
\-A/\-\-skip-orphans explicitly skips forms with missing lemma IDs in
SQLite input; it cannot be used with TSV input.
\-U/\-\-skip-unsupported explicitly skips unsupported multiword or symbol
entries. Skips are counted in build-info.json. The destination must be absent.
.TP
.BI "dev check-plugin " CODE
Report validation checks for an installed pack. Failed checks cause a
nonzero exit status.
.TP
.BI "dev expand-plugin " "CODE ADDITIONAL_DIRECTORY"
Merge additional dictionary pairs into an installed pack, preserving
existing pairs, frequencies, settings and attribution.
\-v/\-\-pack-version VERSION sets the merged version (default 0.2.0).
.TP
.BI "dev package-plugin " CODE
Create a validated pack archive. \-o/\-\-output-dir DIRECTORY selects the
destination (default release-assets).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BLITZER_CONFIG
Explicit configuration file, overridden by \-\-config and ignored by
\-\-no-config.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
User configuration root; defaults to ~/.config.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Language-pack data root; defaults to ~/.local/share.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Sentence-history root; defaults to ~/.local/state.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/bltzr/bltzr.toml
Home configuration file, searched automatically.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bltzr/bltzr.toml
Selected user configuration when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is set.
.TP
.I config.example.toml
Commented configuration example in the source distribution.
.TP
.I PATH.bak
Previous word-list contents when an existing file is rewritten by
cleanup-known --apply or a known-word update.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show basic words (lexeme/lemma), but skip only the listed exact words
(word form):
.PP
.nf
bltzr blitz -l eng -L --skip-exact-words-file exact.txt \\
  -t "I am here. They are here."
.fi
.PP
Skip the entire to be family by writing be in families.txt:
.PP
.nf
bltzr blitz -l eng --skip-word-families-file families.txt \\
  -t "I am here. They were here."
.fi
.PP
Use both lists and export surviving counts and examples:
.PP
.nf
bltzr blitz -l eng --skip-exact-words-file exact.txt \\
  --skip-word-families-file families.txt \\
  -i chapter.txt -f -c -o tsv > words.tsv
.fi
.PP
Process piped input without reading configuration:
.PP
.nf
cat chapter.txt | bltzr blitz -n -l base -o json
.fi
.PP
Preview additions, then explicitly add encountered spellings to the exact list:
.PP
.nf
bltzr blitz -l eng --skip-exact-words-file exact.txt \\
  -i chapter.txt --update-list exact-words -T
bltzr blitz -l eng --skip-exact-words-file exact.txt \\
  -i chapter.txt --update-list exact-words -u
.fi
.PP
Save examples and inspect history:
.PP
.nf
bltzr blitz -l eng -L -t "He helps me." -H
bltzr history eng help --limit 20
.fi
.PP
Export configured instructions and source text for use elsewhere:
.PP
.nf
bltzr blitz -l slv -i chapter.txt -o report -p -s -c
.fi
.PP
Install or update a registered language pack:
.PP
.nf
bltzr install-plugin slv
bltzr install-plugin slv --replace
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 indicates success, including an empty vocabulary result.
1 indicates an application or data error, or an aborted operation.
2 indicates invalid command-line usage.
.SH SEE ALSO
